<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yusuf Karamanli</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1766">1766</a>–<a href="/years/1838">1838</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yusuf Karamanli was pasha of Tripoli from 1795 to 1832, the most powerful ruler of the Karamanli dynasty. He drew wealth from corsairing and tribute from European states, and his demands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> led to the First <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> War of 1801 to 1805. He annexed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fezzan">Fezzan</a>, but debts and civil war forced him to abdicate in 1832.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yusuf-karamanli.jpg" alt="Yusuf Karamanli" width="320" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PortraitVanYusufKaramanli.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yusuf ibn Ali Karamanli, also written Caramanli or Qaramanli, was born in Tripoli in 1766 into the Karamanli dynasty, which took its name from the Karamanids in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and was of Turkish origin. In 1793 the Ottoman officer Ali Pasha deposed his brother Hamet Karamanli, exiled the family and claimed Ottoman rule over Tripoli. In 1795 Hamet and Yusuf returned and, with the help of Hammuda Pasha of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>, recaptured the city. Yusuf then exiled his brother to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> and took the throne himself, becoming the longest-reigning pasha of his house.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1796 he signed the Treaty of Tripoli, which allowed American merchant ships to pass through Tripolitanian waters. In 1801 he is said to have broken it by demanding a tribute of $225,000 from President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a>, who refused, trusting in the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-navy">United States Navy</a>. In May 1801 the pasha declared war by having the flagpole with the American flag cut down before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> consulate. A squadron blockaded Tripoli from 1803, and his forces captured the grounded frigate USS Philadelphia in October 1803 before a raid under Lieutenant Stephen Decatur burned it in the harbour.</p><p>After the defeat at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derna">Derna</a>, and with the American officer and consul William Eaton recruiting his deposed brother Hamet, he capitulated and signed a treaty with Tobias Lear that ended the war on 10 June 1805. On 5 September 1817, after a dispute over tribute and an uprising of the al-Jawazi tribe, he invited its leaders to his castle in Benghazi and ordered the execution of them and of the rest of the tribe; at least 10,000 people were killed, and survivors fled, many to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>By 1819 the treaties of the Napoleonic Wars had forced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> states to give up corsairing almost entirely, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli-greece">Tripoli</a>'s economy began to fail. Yusuf tried to make up the lost revenue by encouraging the trans-Saharan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, without success. Factions formed around his three sons, and after his abdication in favour of his son Ali II civil war followed. Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-ii">Mahmud II</a> sent troops, ostensibly to restore order, who deposed and exiled Ali II, ending both the dynasty and an independent Tripoli. Yusuf died in 1838.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yusuf Karamanli” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Karamanli pashas lived from corsairing, tribute from European powers and the trans-Saharan trade through the Fezzan, which <b>Yusuf Karamanli</b> annexed in 1812.</blockquote><a href="/state/karamanli-tripoli">Karamanli Tripoli</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1812 Muhammad al-Mukni, acting for <b>Yusuf Karamanli</b> of Karamanli Tripoli, killed the last Awlad Muhammad sultan and annexed the Fezzan.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-fezzan">Sultanate of Fezzan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tripoli, in what is now <a href="/countries/libya">Libya</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.89,13.19&amp;year=1812" aria-label="Tripoli on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4888 969 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Yusuf Karamanli"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5338 1239h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5338 1239h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.89,13.19&amp;year=1812">What was here in 1812? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
